<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sanhaja confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1040 CE (approx.) · capital Awdaghust</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The veiled camel-herding Sanhaja tribes of the western Sahara, masters of the caravan routes and forerunners of the Almoravids.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massufa">Massufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudala">Guddala</a> (Juddala), were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> nomads of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>. Their men wore the face veil, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> still do. By the 9th century they had formed a confederation controlling the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, centred on the trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awdaghast">Awdaghust</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The confederation was led by chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a>, and its power rose and fell with its unity. According to the 11th-century geographer al-Bakri, the Lamtuna king Tinbarutan in the mid-10th century ruled a large territory and received tribute from Sudanese kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> grew rich from caravan taxes, livestock and the salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a>, and had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, though their practice was loosely observed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By around 1000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awdaghast">Awdaghust</a> had come under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, and the confederation broke apart. Around 1040 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudala">Guddala</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ibrahim">Yahya ibn Ibrahim</a> brought the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-yasin">Abdallah ibn Yasin</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>, whose reform movement united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> anew as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 800 CE – 1040 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:4.78%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1040 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Awdaghust</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tinbarutan, Yahya ibn Ibrahim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber (Zenaga)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mauritania, Western Sahara</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lamtuna, Sanhaja of the Sahara, Awdaghust kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
